# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
berkeley.md:1: argue/readings: S11 is read 2 ways: berkeley.logic and berkeley.logic.2 have "¬beasts_abstract ∧ ¬brutes_attain_general"; berkeley.logic.3 has "¬beasts_abstract"; they differ when only "the faculties of brutes attain to the having of general ideas" is true, which the second allows
berkeley.md:1: argue/answered: S11: answered yes, which keeps berkeley.logic.3, to: Suppose "the faculties of brutes attain to the having of general ideas" is true and "the power of abstracting is in beasts" is false. Is S11, "I readily agree with this learned author, that the faculties of brutes can by no means attain to ABSTRACTION.", true then?
berkeley.md:1: argue/readings: S18 is read 2 ways: berkeley.logic has "words_general ∧ words_signs_particulars"; berkeley.logic.2 and berkeley.logic.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "words are general" is true, which the second allows
berkeley.md:1: argue/answered: S18: answered no, which keeps berkeley.logic, to: Suppose "words are general" is true and "a word becomes general by being made the sign of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind" is false. Is S18, "But it seems that a word becomes general by being made the sign, not of an ABSTRACT general idea, but of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind.", true then?
berkeley.logic: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: berkeley.logic has "use_words" and "words_signs_particulars → ¬words_signs_general_ideas"; berkeley.logic.2 and berkeley.logic.3 have "use_words"; they differ when only "words are made the signs of general ideas", "men make use of words" and "a word becomes general by being made the sign of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind" are true, which the second allows
berkeley.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: berkeley.logic has "¬words_signs_general_ideas"; berkeley.logic.2 and berkeley.logic.3 have "men_abstract"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
berkeley.logic: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: berkeley.logic puts it at S18, "But it seems that a word becomes general by being made the sign, not of an ABSTRACT general idea, but of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind."; berkeley.logic.2 and berkeley.logic.3 at S14, "From which it follows that men who use language are able to ABSTRACT or GENERALIZE their ideas."
berkeley.logic: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps berkeley.logic, to: Is the text's main conclusion S18, "But it seems that a word becomes general by being made the sign, not of an ABSTRACT general idea, but of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind.", rather than S14, "From which it follows that men who use language are able to ABSTRACT or GENERALIZE their ideas."?
berkeley.logic: argue/readings: U1: berkeley.logic takes for granted "if a word becomes general by being made the sign of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind, then it is not so that words are made the signs of general ideas"; berkeley.logic.2 and berkeley.logic.3 do not
berkeley.logic: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps berkeley.logic, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if a word becomes general by being made the sign of several particular ideas, any one of which it indifferently suggests to the mind, then it is not so that words are made the signs of general ideas"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
